Here is the full process to push your Docker images to a Docker Registry, using the same flow we followed for your devops-exam-portal.
The idea is:

Source Code
    |
    v
Docker Build
    |
    v
Local Docker Image
    |
    v
Docker Tag (Registry Name)
    |
    v
Docker Login
    |
    v
Docker Push
    |
    v
Docker Registry

Step 1: Verify your application images exist locally
After running:
docker compose up -d --build

Docker created images.
First check:

docker images

You saw images like:
REPOSITORY                         TAG
devops-exam-portal-backend         latest
devops-exam-portal-frontend        latest
mysql                              8.0

At this stage:
Images exist only on your EC2 machine
Nobody else can pull them
Example:
EC2 Docker Engine

devops-exam-portal-backend:latest
devops-exam-portal-frontend:latest

Step 2: Create repositories in Docker Registry
I chose Docker Hub as the example.
Create repositories:

Docker Hub
 |
 ├── davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend
 |
 └── davidtejusvi/devops-exam-frontend

Why?
Docker push requires this format:

username/repository:tag

Example:
davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend:v1

Step 3: Login to Docker Registry
Command:
docker login

Docker asks:
Username:
Password:

After successful login:
Login Succeeded

Now Docker knows where to upload.
Step 4: Tag the local image
This is the important step.
Your local image:

devops-exam-portal-backend

does not know where to go.
We attach a registry name:

Command:

docker tag devops-exam-portal-backend davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend:v1

Meaning:
LOCAL IMAGE
     |
     |
     v
REGISTRY IMAGE NAME


devops-exam-portal-backend
          |
          |
          v
davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend:v1

Now check:
docker images

Output:
REPOSITORY                              TAG

devops-exam-portal-backend              latest

davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend        v1

Important:
The image is not copied.

Docker only creates another name/tag pointing to the same image layers.

Step 5: Tag frontend image
Same process:
Original:

devops-exam-portal-frontend

Command:
docker tag devops-exam-portal-frontend davidtejusvi/devops-exam-frontend:v1

Now:
Local:

devops-exam-portal-frontend:latest


Registry:

davidtejusvi/devops-exam-frontend:v1

Step 6: Push images
Now upload.
Backend:

docker push davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend:v1

Docker does:
Check image layers

Layer 1 ───── upload
Layer 2 ───── upload
Layer 3 ───── upload

Create manifest

Finish

Example output:
v1: digest: sha256:xxxxx
status: pushed

Frontend:
docker push davidtejusvi/devops-exam-frontend:v1

Step 7: Verify in registry
Go to Docker Hub.
You should see:

Repositories

davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend

Tags:
    v1


davidtejusvi/devops-exam-frontend

Tags:
    v1

Step 8: Test pulling from another machine
On another server:
Login:

docker login

Pull:
docker pull davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend:v1

Check:
docker images

Now the image exists there.
Step 9: Change docker-compose.yml for production
Before:
backend:
  build: ./backend

Meaning:
EC2 downloads code
      |
      v
Build image
      |
      v
Run container

After registry:
backend:
  image: davidtejusvi/devops-exam-backend:v1

Meaning:
EC2
 |
 |
Pull image from registry
 |
 |
Run container

Same for frontend:
frontend:
  image: davidtejusvi/devops-exam-frontend:v1

Step 10: Deployment becomes simpler
New server:
git clone repo
cd devops-exam-portal
docker compose pull
docker compose up -d

No:
docker build

No:
npm install

No:
mvn package

because everything is already inside the image.
Your complete DevOps pipeline now
